  "goal": "Implement the developer-approved organization of Spec authoring documentation. Make Spec writing guidelines the overall entry, with two linked, separately maintained parts: Required format (machine-checkable structural and syntax rules, existing protocol/format.md) and Writing guidance (content requiring reader/editor judgment, existing protocol/module.md currently titled Module specifications). Add a concise source overview page (e.g. protocol/writing.md), rename the human-facing Module specifications chapter to Writing guidance, retain existing file paths/anchors where practical, and group the docsite navigation under the new overview with the two parts. Explain that semantic writing requirements still matter and mandatory terms retain force; do not call this nondeterministic or optional advice, and do not invent new conformance checks or weaken existing promises. Update source references, templates, README/navigation and common author/reviewer prompts coherently. Ensure workers receive the intended two-part guide in their generated brief and inspect existing packaging/guide tests; retain installed guide path .concorde/protocol/kinds/module.md unless a real need exists. Preserve the previous lightweight workflow and diagram guidance. Update affected Spec descriptions if the rendered guide composition changes, and adjust existing tests that assert the old chapter title or guide content; no new tests merely for editorial wording. Read DEVELOPING.md, canonical principles and complete affected Specs. Prepare task worktree, refresh Protocol manifest/bound assets through prescribed commands, format applicable changed code, run build --check, spec-validation, relevant publication/guide checks and required full Python suite. Avoid unnecessary extra whole-site test repetitions beyond required gates. Commit, task-validation, delivery and concorde_report; main session reviews and merges. The user has approved this documentation organization, routine naming and navigation decisions are within scope.",
